  SimpleStack
    Header 2|Advanced Data Binding
    Text This IWML contains two datasets, Purchases and Vendors declared as literal JSON text.
    PreText
      Text:
        [off]Data[]
          Data:Purchases
            Text:
              [{"ID":3, "Item":"Rabbit Food", "Amount":29.99, "VendorID":3}
              ,{"ID":1, "Item":"Candy", "Amount":1.50, "VendorID":1}
              ,{"ID":2, "Item":"Toilet Seat", "Amount":99.99, "VendorID":2}
              ,{"ID":4, "Item":"Light Bulbs", "Amount":9.49, "VendorID":1}
              ,{"ID":5, "Item":"Sink", "Amount":75.00, "VendorID":2}]
          Data:Vendors
            Text:
              [{"ID":1, "Name":"Amazoon"}
              ,{"ID":2, "Name":"zBay"}
              ,{"ID":3, "Name":"Home Despot"}]
          Data:Users https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users

    Header 3|ListBox
    Text This ListBox is completely databound with three simple expressions.
    PreText Items: =Rows(Order:asc).Vendors.Name
    Text First, the items displayed to the user are shown. This expression specifies to use field Name from the Vendors dataset and order in in ascending order.
    PreText Values: =Rows(Order:asc Sort:Name).Vendors.ID
    Text Second, the values used for lookup to a second dateset are specific. This expression specifies to use field ID from the Vendors dataset, and but order by Name in in ascending order.
    PreText Value: =.Purchases.VendorID
    Text Third, the expression definfes where to retrieve and store the value to. This expression specifies the VendorID field in the Purchases dataset.
    ListBox
      Value: =.Purchases.VendorID
      Items: =Rows(Order:asc).Vendors.Name
      Values: =Rows(Order:asc Sort:Name).Vendors.ID
    Text Next we specify simple data bindings with:
    PreText
      Text:
        [off]=.Purchases.Item
        =.Purchases.Amount
    Text Item:
    Edit =.Purchases.Item
    Text Item:
    Edit =.Purchases.Amount

    Header 3|Data Cursors
    Text Each dataset maintains its own cursor. You can use these buttons to move forward and backward.
    Text Note how the bound controls above, including the ListBox update automatically.
    Button:PrevButton Previous
    Button:NextButton Next

    Header 3|Row Binding
    Text Rows that are not pointed to by the cursor can also be bound to. Despite the lookups, the bindings remain two way.
    PreText [off]=.Vendors.Name
    Text Row pointed to by cursor.
    Edit =.Vendors.Name
    PreText [off]=.Vendors(Key:3).Name
    Text Row with Key=3. In Vendors, ID is the Key.
    Edit =.Vendors(Key:3).Name
    PreText [off]=.Vendors(Index:1).Name
    Text Second row in Vendors (Index 1)
    Edit =.Vendors(Index:1).Name

    Header 3|Nested Data
    Text Nested data can easily be accessed as well.
    PreText Text =.Users.name
    Text Name: [=.Users.name]
    PreText Text =.Users.address.city
    Text =.Users.address.city
    PreText Text Coordinates [=.Users.address.geo.lat] : [=.Users.address.geo.lng]
    Text Coordinates [=.Users.address.geo.lat] : [=.Users.address.geo.lng]

    Header 3|Page Binding
    Text You can even auomatically enable/disable the buttons using data binding.
    Text Note: This syntax will be changing in the near future.
    PreText Enabled: =page.Users.BOF:!
    Button:PrevUsersButton Previous
      Enabled: =page.Users.BOF:!
    PreText Enabled: =page.Users.EOF:!
    Button:NextUsersButton Next
      Enabled: =page.Users.EOF:!
    Text The : specifies a modifier. There are several available, but here we use ! which peforms a boolean inversion on the previous identifier.
